WEBVTT
TITLE Context Free Grammars And Pushdown Automata — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A context-free grammar has four parts: nonterminals, terminals, rules, and a start symbol.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
A derivation replaces one nonterminal at a time. S becomes a S b, then a a S b b, then a a b b.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
A leftmost derivation always expands the leftmost nonterminal. Every derivation has a tree, and the tree is what ambiguity is about.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Context-free means the left side of every rule is a single nonterminal, so a replacement never inspects its neighbours.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
The grammar E to E plus E, E to E star E, E to id gives the string id plus id star id two different trees.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
That is ambiguity: one string, two distinct trees. Precedence is the usual repair, and it is a change to the grammar.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Ambiguity is a property of a grammar. A language is inherently ambiguous when no grammar for it is unambiguous.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Counting trees is mechanical: the number of binary bracketings of n operators is the Catalan number.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Three cleanups come first: drop useless symbols, then epsilon rules, then unit rules.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
In Chomsky normal form every rule is A to B C or A to a. Nothing else is allowed.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Binarising makes every tree binary, so a string of length n has exactly 2n minus 1 nodes on its derivation.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Two tools need this form: the CYK membership test, and the pumping lemma for context-free languages.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
A pushdown automaton is a finite machine plus a stack. Seven parts, and the stack is the only new one.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
One move reads one input symbol or nothing, pops the stack top, and pushes a string in its place.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
An instantaneous description records the state, the unread input, and the whole stack.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Last in, first out: the stack can compare a prefix with the reverse of a later block, and nothing else.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
For a to the n b to the n: push one marker per a, pop one per b, accept when both are exhausted together.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Watch a a b b: the stack grows to two markers, then shrinks to the bottom marker, and the input ends at the same moment.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Acceptance by final state and by empty stack are equivalent for nondeterministic machines, and not for deterministic ones.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Deterministic pushdown machines are strictly weaker: w w reversed is context-free, and no deterministic machine accepts it.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
The mirror language works because pushing and popping reverses. The copy language fails for the same reason.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
a to the n b to the n c to the n needs two comparisons, and one stack is spent after the first.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
a to the n b to the n c to the m and a to the m b to the n c to the n are both context-free, and their intersection is not.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
The one-line test: count how many independent quantities must be compared at once. One is context-free, two usually is not.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Context-free languages are closed under union, concatenation, star, reversal, and intersection with a regular language.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Two operations do not: intersection, and complement.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
The useful exception: if one of the two languages is regular, the intersection is context-free.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Deterministic context-free languages are closed under complement. The whole class is not, and w w proves it.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
The pumping lemma: a long enough string splits into five parts, and the two middle pieces can be repeated together.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Why two pieces: a binary tree repeats a nonterminal on a path, and that repeat carries two subtrees side by side.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Refuting a to the n b to the n c to the n: take a to the p b to the p c to the p and check every legal split.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
What it cannot do: satisfying the lemma never proves a language context-free.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Shape one: four languages, one is not context-free. Test each by the number of comparisons.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Shape two: a union and an intersection of the same pair. Union yes, intersection no.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Shape three: a multiple-select cluster of statements about determinism and complements.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Shape four: read a grammar back to its language. Shape five: which of four languages are context-free.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: read the language as a set of comparisons, and count the independent ones.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: try to build. A grammar or a machine settles the question, and you can stop.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step three: if the build fails, pump. Choose a string with the right counts, then refute every split.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Step four: check the quantifiers, the empty string, and whether the question says deterministic.

